--TEST--
Every way a response body is produced agrees that a status set in front of it changes nothing, `echo` included -- spec § 15 and `rule:tooling/echo-always-has-a-sink` and `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, asked of the whole roster at once: the question is not what each
// writer produced but that a `setStatus` before it left that answer alone. A
// member that folded the status it was given into its own bytes would fail
// here while still looking entirely right on its own line.
var $echoed = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    Core\Response::setStatus(201);
    echo "body";
});
var $texted = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    Core\Response::setStatus(202);
    Core\Response::text("body");
});
var $octets = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    Core\Response::setStatus(203);
    Core\Response::bytes("body" as bytes, "application/octet-stream");
});

// Counted rather than read off a line, so three writers that each answered
// plausibly but differently still fail. Interpolated into a `string` first:
// `Core\Out::capture` answers a `Core\Cli\Text` carrier, and `rule:expressions/object-identity-equality`
// makes `==` between two objects identity -- comparing the carriers directly
// answers "differ" for two captures holding the same bytes.
string $echoedText = "{$echoed}";
string $textedText = "{$texted}";
string $octetsText = "{$octets}";
uint $agreed = 0;
if ($echoedText == $textedText) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}
if ($textedText == $octetsText) {
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}
echo "agreed ", $agreed, " of 2 on [", $echoedText, "]\n";

// `json` is the one that may not agree, and that is § 4 rather than the
// status: it frames the value, so a string comes back as a JSON string. The
// status in front of it is invisible here too.
var $encoded = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    Core\Response::setStatus(200);
    Core\Response::json("body");
});
echo "json wrote ", $encoded, "\n";

// The status of the last call is what the response would carry, and a `.nvst`
// case cannot see a status line -- `nvs_server::serve`'s own tests pin that
// half, which is where the two declarations become what the peer sees.
var $lastWins = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    Core\Response::setStatus(500);
    Core\Response::setStatus(200);
    echo "body";
});
echo "two statuses wrote [", $lastWins, "]\n";
--EXPECT--
agreed 2 of 2 on [body]
json wrote "body"
two statuses wrote [body]
